  "evaluation": "B is best: it correctly identifies an apparent conflict because Study B measures a single depression dimension within a quality-of-life instrument, whereas Study A uses the full HDRS, matching the rules for outcome_measure. It also recognizes that Study B explicitly reports improvement, not harm. A likewise correctly notices the reversed direction in the supplied harm label, but rests its classification on scale direction rather than the explicitly permitted distinction between constructs measured. C is weakest: it treats the findings as opposite despite Study B's stated improvement and misses the outcome-measure distinction that dissolves the conflict.",
